@page "/how-it-works"

<PageTitle>How the engine works · louis-agent</PageTitle>

<section class="band band-movie">
    <span class="kicker">How it works · in detail</span>
    <h2 class="chrome">One turn, through every layer.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Sixty seconds, ten scenes. The six slabs inside core are the real middleware in its real order, outermost first, the way <code>ChatPipeline.cs</code> builds it.</p>
    <FilmFrame><DetailedMovie /></FilmFrame>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">The parts, in plain words, in the order a turn meets them</span>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong>Your program.</strong> A few lines in a project of your own: they name the catalogue, who the agent is and what it can do, and start a host. A demo is such a project, and so is a customer's.</li>
        <li><strong>The hosts, as libraries.</strong> A terminal, Rider's AI chat, an HTTP API with answers streamed as server-sent events, an MCP server that hands the tools to another client. Each maps the agent's stream to its protocol and holds no behaviour of its own.</li>
        <li><strong>The framework's web, and the accounts.</strong> A site is a Blazor WebAssembly app on the framework's web, served by the API from the same origin, reaching the engine only over HTTP. A person signs in through ASP.NET Core Identity with a tenant from the host name, and the key gate takes the sign-in or a key before any session opens.</li>
        <li><strong>The engine.</strong> The loop: streaming, the providers, the usage ledger, handing work to other sessions, and the guards around every tool and fetch. It holds no agent and no tools, and names no product and no country. Everything goes through one <code>IChatClient</code> from Microsoft.Extensions.AI, so the provider is configuration: Claude by default, Ollama or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint by a setting.</li>
        <li><strong>The identity.</strong> Who an agent is: its voice, its modes and its roles, as markdown. Every identity's prompt opens with the same principles file. <a href="/identities">Identities</a></li>
        <li><strong>The packs.</strong> What an agent can do: toolsets and their guides, each pack its own package. A pack needs only the contracts, never the engine, never knows another pack or an identity, and can be served over MCP as it is. <a href="/packs">Packs</a></li>
        <li><strong>The eval harness.</strong> What proves it: every check a C# eval on one harness, kept beside the identity or pack it proves, recording what the model answered and read so a run re-scores for nothing. <a href="/proof">Measured</a></li>
        <li><strong>Where it runs.</strong> Docker on a developer's machine, two Container Apps in Azure released from GitHub after green CI. <a href="/architecture">The whole map</a></li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">The rules every turn keeps</span>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong>An agent runs in a closed Docker container.</strong> It sees only the folders mounted into it and the secrets handed to it. The fetcher it must use refuses your network's internal addresses. Nothing it does reaches the machine it runs on.</li>
        <li><strong>Arguments are data.</strong> Model text never reaches a shell. A tool gets typed arguments.</li>
        <li><strong>The context is guarded.</strong> A result over 50,000 characters is summarised by a separate call. A call cut off by the output limit never runs. A failure goes back to the model with its reason.</li>
        <li><strong>History is append-only.</strong> Nothing edits the system prompt, the tools or earlier messages mid-conversation, so the prompt prefix stays cacheable.</li>
        <li><strong>Every request is measured.</strong> One priced record per model request, from a dated price table. An unknown price stays unknown, never 0.</li>
        <li><strong>A session keeps its tools.</strong> It names its toolsets when it opens, and there is one engine per toolset list, in a fixed order, so the same list always sends the same prefix.</li>
        <li><strong>A pack can't skip a guard.</strong> It asks the engine for a fetcher, which refuses internal addresses and obeys a site's <code>robots.txt</code> on every request.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
